    "body": "@realdevnullius Hello, glad to see you taking an interest.  Basically those trackers are misconfigured and likely sitting behind some sort of proxy or running in a docker container, etc.  When they issue a reply to your client they are replying with addresses that are not useful to you.  A reply of 127.0.0.1 would just redirect your client to query itself.\n\nThe point of a tracker is assist you in reaching other members of the pool and return to you valid addresses either via ipv6 or ipv4.\n\nAlso some are sitting behind cloudflare proxies and are misconfigured only replying with cloudflare addresses.\n\nreturns of 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, 255.255.255.255 are all bogus invalid replies and your client is just wasting time trying to connect to non valid endpoints.\n\nWithout a valid DNS entry you will never be able to connect to the tracker to obtain any information.\n\nPlay around with my tracker tool and you should get a feel for what is legit vs what is junk return data to assist you weeding out bad trackers.  It should be pretty evident of what is bad vs good after playing around for a bit.\n\nTool should work fine on MacOS or any linux variation, I have not tested on wintel python installs.  If you want to run linux but dont have a pc to run linux then just install ubuntu 24.04 or something using virtualbox.\n\nAlso in your torrent client if you really want to test trackers using a torrent client turn off DHT and PEX, force all client discovery through the trackers.\n\n-HR",
    "author_association": "NONE",
